    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Safety first. After Hurricane Laura in 2020, NOAA records three deaths in Port Arthur from carbon monoxide from a generator running in an enclosed area.</li>
<li>A Port Arthur hurricane damage claim often means two or three claims: TWIA for wind, a flood policy for surge and rising water, and a homeowners policy for other covered losses.</li>
<li>Photograph everything before cleanup, make temporary repairs, and keep every receipt. Mold can start quickly in Southeast Texas humidity.</li>
<li>Hurricane and windstorm deductibles are often a percentage of your dwelling limit, so know the dollar amount before the adjuster arrives.</li>
<li>Deadlines differ by policy: TWIA (generally one year to file), NFIP (proof of loss generally within 60 days), and private insurers under Texas Chapter 542, which can be extended 15 days after a declared catastrophe.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>When a hurricane hits the upper Texas coast, Port Arthur homeowners face two jobs at once: getting the house safe and dry, and starting what can be a complicated insurance process. A Port Arthur hurricane damage claim is different from a typical claim in most of Texas because coverage is usually split across separate policies, adjusters arrive in waves from out of state, and the same storm can bring wind, wind-driven rain, storm surge, and days of rainfall flooding.</p>

<p>This guide walks through what to do, in order, from the first hours after the storm through the final payment.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Stay safe after the storm</h2>

<p>No claim is worth an injury. Before you inspect anything:</p>
<ul>
<li>Wait for local officials to say it is safe to return if you evacuated. Follow City of Port Arthur and Jefferson County guidance.</li>
<li>Stay away from downed power lines and standing water that could be energized.</li>
<li>Do not walk on a damaged roof. Photograph from the ground or let a professional inspect.</li>
<li>Run generators outdoors only, well away from windows and doors. During Hurricane Laura, <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=918105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA’s Storm Events Database</a> records five indirect deaths in Jefferson County from carbon monoxide poisoning, including three in Port Arthur from a generator running in an enclosed area.</li>
<li>Watch for structural damage, gas leaks, and wildlife displaced by floodwater.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 2: Document the damage before cleanup</h2>

<p>Evidence is the foundation of every claim. Before you move or throw anything away:</p>
<ul>
<li>Take wide shots of each side of the house, then close-ups of the roof, siding, windows, fences, and outbuildings.</li>
<li>Walk every room with video, including the attic, closets, and garage.</li>
<li>If water came in, photograph the waterline on walls and note whether it came from above (roof, windows) or rose from the ground. That distinction decides which policy responds.</li>
<li>Photograph serial numbers and model tags on damaged appliances and electronics.</li>
<li>Keep samples of damaged materials, such as a piece of carpet, a shingle, or a section of drywall, until the adjuster has seen them.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/storm-damage-documentation-collage.webp" alt="Collage of storm damage photos showing roof, siding, and interior water damage" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Wide shots, close-ups, and video of every affected area make it far easier to prove the full scope of a hurricane loss.</figcaption></figure>

<p>For a full list, see our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> and <a href="/how-to-document-storm-damage-port-arthur/">how to document storm damage in Port Arthur</a>.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Prevent further damage (mitigation)</h2>

<p>Most policies require you to take reasonable steps to protect the property from further damage. Damage that gets worse because nothing was done may not be covered. Once you have photos:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tarp roof openings and board up broken windows and doors.</li>
<li>Extract standing water and start drying with fans and dehumidifiers.</li>
<li>Remove soaked carpet, pad, and drywall where needed, after photographing it.</li>
<li>Move undamaged contents to a dry area.</li>
<li>Keep every receipt and invoice, including for your own time and supplies if you do the work yourself.</li>
</ul>

<p>Speed matters on the Gulf Coast. High heat and humidity mean mold can begin growing within days of a water loss. If drying cannot happen quickly, document why, such as power outages or contractor shortages. See our page on <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>

<p>Hold off on permanent repairs, like a full roof replacement, until the adjuster has inspected, unless the insurer agrees in writing.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Figure out which policies apply</h2>

<p>This is where Port Arthur differs from most of Texas. Jefferson County is a TWIA first-tier coastal county, so many homes carry a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail, a separate TWIA windstorm policy, and a separate flood policy.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type of hurricane damage</th><th>Usually claimed under</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Roof, siding, or windows damaged by wind</td><td>TWIA (or homeowners policy if it includes wind)</td><td>Windstorm or hurricane deductible applies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain entering through wind-damaged roof or windows</td><td>TWIA or wind-covering homeowners policy</td><td>Document the opening the wind created</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm surge or rising floodwater</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td><td>Excluded by homeowners and TWIA policies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tree falling on the house</td><td>Usually the wind policy</td><td>Debris removal limits may apply</td></tr>
<tr><td>Food spoilage, fire, or theft after the storm</td><td>Homeowners policy, depending on terms</td><td>Check sublimits and deductibles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Living elsewhere while repairs happen</td><td>Homeowners or TWIA additional living expense (if purchased)</td><td>NFIP generally does not cover living expenses</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Report the claim to each insurer that may be responsible, and keep claim numbers, adjuster names, and contact details for each in one place. When the same room has wind-driven rain from above and floodwater from below, each adjuster will try to assign damage to the other policy. Careful photos and moisture readings help sort that out. Read <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage in Port Arthur</a> for more on this common dispute.</p>

<h3>Flood claim timing</h3>
<p>For NFIP policies, FEMA guidance says the signed proof of loss is generally due within 60 days of the loss, even if you disagree with the adjuster’s estimate, though FEMA sometimes extends that deadline after major disasters. Confirm the current deadline and submission address with your flood insurer. The <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/claims-checklist" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NFIP claims checklist</a> is a good starting point. Also remember that NFIP policies generally have a 30-day waiting period, so a policy bought as a storm approaches usually won’t cover it.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Step 5: Know your deductibles before the adjuster arrives</h2>

<p>Many Texas policies carry a separate windstorm, named-storm, or hurricane deductible expressed as a percentage of Coverage A, such as 1%, 2%, or 5%. On a $250,000 dwelling limit, a 2% deductible is $5,000, and a 5% deductible is $12,500. Your flood policy has its own deductible. If you have two or three claims from one storm, you may face two or three deductibles.</p>

<p>Knowing the dollar figure helps you decide what to claim and set realistic expectations. Try our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a>, and read <a href="/hurricane-deductible-texas-explained/">Texas hurricane deductibles explained</a>.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Track additional living expenses</h2>

<p>If the home is unlivable after a covered loss, additional living expense (ALE, sometimes called loss of use) coverage may pay the increased cost of living elsewhere, such as hotel or rental costs, extra mileage, laundry, and meals above your normal grocery bill. Keep every receipt, write down why the home could not be occupied, and ask the adjuster early how ALE will be handled. Whether you have ALE through TWIA depends on whether it was purchased on that policy. NFIP flood policies generally don’t include it.</p>

<p>Landlords and businesses should look at loss of rents and business income coverage. Port Arthur’s refinery, petrochemical, and port economy means many local businesses face shutdowns and supply interruptions after a storm. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Step 7: Prepare for catastrophe adjusters and delays</h2>

<p>After a major hurricane, insurers bring in independent catastrophe adjusters from around the country. Expect:</p>
<ul>
<li>Waits for inspections, sometimes weeks, as adjusters work through large caseloads</li>
<li>Files reassigned between adjusters, and repeated requests for the same documents</li>
<li>Estimates written quickly that may miss hidden damage, local pricing, or code-required items</li>
<li>Contractor shortages and price increases that make the estimate harder to repair against</li>
</ul>

<p>Communicate in writing, keep a claim diary with dates and names, and send documents in a way you can prove, such as through the insurer’s portal or by email.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/flooded-living-room-homeowners-adjuster.webp" alt="Homeowners and an adjuster standing in a flood-damaged living room after a hurricane" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>When wind-driven rain and rising water damage the same room, each insurer needs clear evidence of what caused what.</figcaption></figure>

<h3>Texas claim deadlines after a hurricane</h3>
<p>For most private insurers, <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542</a> generally requires the insurer to acknowledge the claim, begin investigating, and request information within 15 days after notice; to accept or reject within 15 business days after receiving everything it requested (or explain why it needs more time, up to 45 more days); and to pay within 5 business days after accepting. After a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster declared by the Commissioner of Insurance, those deadlines can be extended by 15 days. Surplus lines insurers are treated differently.</p>
<p>TWIA is not subject to Chapter 542. It follows its own timelines in Chapter 2210: claims generally must be filed within one year of the date of loss, and TWIA generally has 60 days after receiving the claim or requested information to accept or deny. See our <a href="/twia-windstorm-claim-guide-port-arthur/">TWIA windstorm claim guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Step 8: Review the estimate before you accept it</h2>

<p>When the estimate arrives, compare it line by line with what you see in the house and with contractor bids. Common gaps after Southeast Texas hurricanes include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Roof repairs scoped where replacement is needed, or missing decking and flashing</li>
<li>No allowance for TDI windstorm (WPI-8) requirements on roof work in Jefferson County</li>
<li>Code-required items under ordinance or law coverage, such as items required by Port Arthur’s 2021 International Residential Code</li>
<li>Missing interior damage from wind-driven rain, including wet insulation and drywall</li>
<li>Heavy depreciation, especially on older roofs</li>
<li>Contents left off or undervalued</li>
</ul>

<p>Under a replacement cost policy, the insurer typically pays actual cash value first and releases recoverable depreciation after repairs are done, within a deadline in the policy. Track that deadline. If you find missed items, submit a supplement with photos and estimates.</p>

<h2>Step 9: Plan repairs around permits and windstorm inspections</h2>

<p>Roof replacements and structural repairs in Port Arthur generally need a city permit, and in Jefferson County, roof work and other repairs must meet TDI windstorm requirements and receive a WPI-8 Certificate of Compliance for the home to remain eligible for TWIA coverage. Confirm requirements with the City of Port Arthur Building Inspections Division and use contractors familiar with windstorm inspections. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page has details.</p>

<h2>When to get help</h2>

<p>Many hurricane claims are handled directly with the insurers. Others, especially those with multiple policies, large structural damage, wind-versus-flood disputes, or estimates far below repair bids, can benefit from professional help. A Port Arthur Public Adjuster can document the loss, prepare a detailed Xactimate estimate, coordinate the separate claims, and present them to each insurer. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Legal questions, such as bad faith or lawsuit deadlines, belong with a Texas attorney.</p>

<p>Whether the next storm is a Laura, a Delta, or something smaller, the steps are the same: stay safe, document everything, protect the property, report to every insurer, and read every estimate before you accept it. Learn more on our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page, or ask a Port Arthur Public Adjuster for a free claim review.</p>
